The '''2022 Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022. All 51 members of [[Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council]] were elected. The elections took place alongside [[2022 London local elections|local elections in the other London boroughs]] and [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|elections to local authorities across the United Kingdom]].

In the [[2018 Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council election|previous election in 2018]], the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] maintained its longstanding control of the council, winning all fifty-one seats. The 2022 election took place under new election boundaries with the Labour Party once again winning all seats.

On 15th September 2025, three Labour Councillors resigned from the party and joined the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]]. They formed the first opposition group on the Council since 2006.<ref>https://opencouncildata.co.uk/council.php?c=227&y=0</ref>

==Background==
===History===
[[File:Barking and Dagenham UK local election 2010 map.svg|thumb|Result of the 2018 borough election]]
The thirty-two [[London boroughs]] were established in 1965 by the [[London Government Act 1963]]. They are the principal authorities in Greater London and have responsibilities including education, housing, planning, highways, social services, libraries, recreation, waste, environmental health and revenue collection. Some of the powers are shared with the [[Greater London Authority]], which also manages passenger transport, police and fire.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The essential guide to London local government {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/essential-guide-london-local-government|access-date=2021-10-10|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref>

Barking and Dagenham has been controlled by the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] since its establishment. The [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]], [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]], [[Independent politician|independents]] and [[residents associations]] have also held seats on the borough. The [[2006 Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council election|2006 election]] saw the [[British National Party]] winning twelve of the fifty-one seats, none of which they held in the [[2010 Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council election|2010 election]], in which every seat was won by Labour.<ref>{{Cite web|last=King|first=Jonathan|date=2021-09-02|title=Tributes to former mayor of Barking and Dagenham who has died aged 83|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/tributes-to-inder-singh-jamu-barking-dagenham-mayor-8290224|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref> In the [[2014 Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council election|2014]] election and the most recent [[2018 Barking and Dagenham London Borough Council election|election in 2018]] all fifty-one seats continued to be won by the Labour Party.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Horton|first=Tom|date=2018-05-04|title=Labour sweep to victory in Barking and Dagenham Council elections by taking all 51 seats|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/labour-sweep-to-victory-in-barking-and-dagenham-council-elections-3360088|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref> The party won 74.4% of the vote across the borough.<ref name=":0" /> The Conservatives won 23.6% of the vote and no seats. The incumbent leader of the council is the Labour councillor Darren Rodwell, who had held that position since 2014.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web|last=Horton|first=Tom|date=2018-05-14|title=Leader of Barking and Dagenham Council reshuffles his cabinet|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/local-council/news-politics-leader-barking-and-dagenham-council-shuffles-his-cabinet-3359614|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref>

===Council term===
Bill Turner, a councillor for [[Thames (Barking and Dagenham ward)|Thames]] ward, resigned in 2021. A by-election to replace him was held in 2021 alongside [[2021 London mayoral election|that year's mayoral election]] and [[2021 London Assembly election|London Assembly election]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=King|first=Jonathan|date=2021-04-13|title=Meet the candidates battling for votes in the Thames ward by-election|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/meet-thames-ward-by-election-candidates-7891708|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref> The London Assembly member [[Andrew Boff]] was selected as the Conservative candidate.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Baston|first=Lewis|date=2021-03-20|title=Lewis Baston: A colourful rush of London borough by-elections is coming|url=https://www.onlondon.co.uk/lewis-baston-a-colourful-rush-of-london-borough-by-elections-is-coming/|access-date=2021-09-14|website=OnLondon|language=en-GB}}</ref> He called the election a "referendum on democracy", expressing his opposition to the Labour council's introduction of a [[Controlled Parking Zone]] in the ward.<ref>{{Cite web|last=King|first=Jonathan|date=2021-03-18|title=Tory candidate calls Thames ward by-election 'referendum on democracy'|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/andrew-boff-on-thames-ward-by-election-7827052|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref> The Labour candidate Fatuma Nalule won the election, with Boff coming second.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Boyle|first=Caislin|date=2021-05-10|title=Thames ward by-election winner thanks voters for support|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/new-labour-councillor-joins-barking-and-dagenham-ranks-7959112|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref>

Along with most other London boroughs, Barking and Dagenham was subject to a boundary review ahead of the 2022 election. The [[Local Government Boundary Commission for England]] concluded that the council should maintain fifty-one seats, but produced new election boundaries following a period of consultation.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Cox|first=Michael|date=2021-03-29|title=Two extra wards for Barking and Dagenham in boundary change plans|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/barking-and-dagenham-ward-boundary-change-plans-7852192|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref> The plans will see two additional wards, resulting in six of the nineteen new wards being represented by two councillors and the rest being represented by three councillors.<ref>{{Cite web|last=King|first=Jonathan|date=2021-09-07|title=Final plan to change Barking and Dagenham ward boundaries revealed|url=https://www.barkinganddagenhampost.co.uk/news/barking-dagenham-final-ward-boundary-changes-8297150|access-date=2021-09-14|website=Barking and Dagenham Post|language=en-UK}}</ref> Boundaries changed for every ward except for Longbridge.<ref>{{cite web|title=The Local Government Boundary Commission for England|url=https://s3-eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/lgbce/B%26D%20Summary.pdf|date=31 August 2021}}</ref>

==Electoral process==
Barking and Dagenham, as is the case all other London borough councils, elects all of its councillors at once every four years, with the previous election having taken place in 2018. The election took place by multi-member [[first-past-the-post voting]], with each ward being represented by two or three councillors. Electors will have as many votes as there are councillors to be elected in their ward, with the top two or three being elected.

All [[Elections in the United Kingdom#Registration procedure|registered electors]] ([[British citizen|British]], [[Irish citizen|Irish]], [[Commonwealth citizen|Commonwealth]] and [[European Union]] citizens) living in London aged 18 or over were entitled to vote in the election. People who lived at two addresses in different councils, such as university students with different term-time and holiday addresses, were entitled to be registered for and vote in elections in both local authorities.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=How the elections work {{!}} London Councils|url=https://www.londoncouncils.gov.uk/who-runs-london/london-elections/how-elections-work|access-date=2021-09-14|website=www.londoncouncils.gov.uk}}</ref> Voting in-person at polling stations took place from 7:00 to 22:00 on election day, and voters were able to apply for postal votes or proxy votes in advance of the election.<ref name=":1" />

